| Dropbox-merge duplicate-row ledger repair + lock-safe live apply | `dedup_fills_ledger.py` fixes a specific corruption class any Dropbox-synced, live-appended CSV can suffer: a conflict-merge duplicating a whole leading block of rows (not byte-identical - the later copy carries extra trailing empty fields from a wider later schema, so a naive exact-match dedup finds zero of them; `_strip_trailing_empty_fields()` normalizes before comparing), plus an embedded literal header row sitting as a data value. Mutation-tested; `dedup_file()` refuses to ever overwrite its own source (`SameFileError` + exclusive `open(path,"x")` creation) after a review caught that hole. `apply_dedup_safely.py` is the safe-apply half - imports the live bot's own `exclusive_state_lock()` directly (confirmed import-safe: all side-effecting code sits behind `if __name__=="__main__":`) and holds it for the whole read-dedup-verify-replace sequence, so a fill can never land mid-swap; writes a timestamped backup first, verifies the kept lines are an in-order subsequence of the original before trusting the result, supports `--dry-run`. Any project with a live-written ledger/state file inside (or formerly inside) a Dropbox-synced tree should check for this exact corruption shape before trusting row counts, and can reuse both scripts directly. | `Sam Wills/0.1---GIT HUB---/The Aggressive Trading Game/Staging/g38-dedup-20260910/{dedup_fills_ledger,apply_dedup_safely}.py` | The Aggressive Trading Game | ai-infra, trading | Live; applied to both razor and sb5k live ledgers (1,410/1,282 rows removed), zero data loss | 2026-09-11 |
